WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:10.271
Sound familiar? You put your stop just below a low, price spikes right into it – and then turns in your direction. That's not bad luck. That's liquidity.

00:00:10.271 --> 00:00:22.527
In this video we'll look at where liquidity sits on the chart, how a stop hunt plays out, why equal highs and equal lows matter so much – and how to tell a sweep from a real breakout.

00:00:22.527 --> 00:00:33.268
Important up front: this is educational content, not investment advice. Trading involves risk of loss. Practise new strategies on a demo account first.

00:00:36.068 --> 00:00:45.869
Liquidity sounds complicated, but it's simple: it's the places on the chart where a lot of orders are waiting. And most of them are stop-loss orders.

00:00:45.869 --> 00:00:51.438
The market rises and forms a clear high. Everyone sees it, everyone draws it.

00:00:51.438 --> 00:00:54.864
Then comes a pullback, and a clear low forms.

00:00:54.864 --> 00:01:01.138
Above the high, the sellers' stops pile up – plus the buy orders of traders waiting for a breakout.

00:01:01.138 --> 00:01:06.942
Below the low sit the buyers' stops. Exactly where most textbooks tell you to put your stop.

00:01:06.942 --> 00:01:20.434
Why does that matter? Big market players need someone on the other side of their positions. Triggered stops provide exactly those orders. That's why these areas often act like a magnet for price.

00:01:23.234 --> 00:01:30.579
Now let's look at what happens when price goes and takes that liquidity. This is called a stop hunt or liquidity sweep.

00:01:30.579 --> 00:01:33.274
The market drops and forms a low.

00:01:33.274 --> 00:01:39.914
Price comes back to roughly the same level once more. Now there are a lot of stops sitting below these lows.

00:01:39.914 --> 00:01:45.718
Then a candle spikes below with a long wick. The stops get triggered – that's the sweep.

00:01:45.718 --> 00:01:55.388
What matters is what happens next: price immediately returns above the old low. The sellers who got stopped out or went short there are now on the wrong side.

00:01:55.388 --> 00:02:05.901
Often a strong impulse follows in the other direction. But careful: a sweep alone is not a trade idea yet. It's only the first clue.

00:02:08.701 --> 00:02:17.797
A particularly strong magnet is equal highs and equal lows. Many traders see them as safe resistance. In reality it's often the opposite.

00:02:17.797 --> 00:02:20.544
Here gold rises and forms a high.

00:02:20.544 --> 00:02:27.419
After a pullback, price runs right back up to the same level – and turns away again. A textbook double top.

00:02:27.419 --> 00:02:35.235
Now there are twice as many stops and breakout orders above this line. The cleaner the highs, the more liquidity builds up there.

00:02:35.235 --> 00:02:43.964
And then it happens: a wick shoots above the highs, takes the stops – but the candle closes back below. The breakout buyers are trapped.

00:02:43.964 --> 00:02:52.145
Shortly after, price breaks below the last low. A break of structure to the downside confirms that the sellers have taken over.

00:02:52.145 --> 00:03:01.170
The sell-off often keeps going until it reaches the next liquidity below. With equal lows, it works exactly the other way round.

00:03:03.970 --> 00:03:12.699
Of course, not every break above a high is a sweep. Sometimes the breakout is real. So the key question is: how do you tell the difference?

00:03:12.699 --> 00:03:18.634
Again we see several highs at the same level. Here too, liquidity sits above the line.

00:03:18.634 --> 00:03:25.509
This time a candle with a big body closes clearly above the highs. No long wick, no quick drop back.

00:03:25.509 --> 00:03:32.984
Price comes back to the old line but stays above it. The former resistance now holds as support. That's called a retest.

00:03:32.984 --> 00:03:42.602
After that, the move continues. That's the difference: with a sweep, price returns quickly. With a real breakout, it closes above and holds the level.

00:03:42.602 --> 00:03:51.679
If you're unsure, wait. One or two candles of patience cost you little – an entry in the wrong direction costs more.

00:03:54.479 --> 00:03:56.025
So remember:

00:03:56.025 --> 00:04:01.123
With a sweep, only the wick spikes beyond the level, and price comes back quickly.

00:04:01.123 --> 00:04:06.543
With a breakout, the candle closes beyond it, and the level holds.

00:04:09.343 --> 00:04:16.819
Finally, let's put it all together. This is how a sweep can become the starting point for a well-thought-out trade idea.

00:04:16.819 --> 00:04:22.335
The five-minute chart is correcting lower, while the higher-timeframe trend points up.

00:04:22.335 --> 00:04:27.225
The stops sit below the last low. A wick takes them out – the sweep.

00:04:27.225 --> 00:04:33.577
Then a candle closes above the last lower high. The change of character shows that the buyers are back.

00:04:33.577 --> 00:04:39.669
The impulse leaves a fair value gap in the discount area. That's where you wait for the pullback.

00:04:39.669 --> 00:04:47.327
The entry comes in the gap, and the stop goes below the sweep low. The market has already taken the liquidity there.

00:04:47.327 --> 00:04:57.475
If the plan works, the next target is often the liquidity above the highs. But this setup can fail too – the stop always belongs in the plan.

00:05:00.275 --> 00:05:01.847
Let's sum up.

00:05:01.847 --> 00:05:06.867
Liquidity sits where lots of stops sit: above highs and below lows.

00:05:06.867 --> 00:05:12.358
Equal highs and equal lows aren't safe resistance – they're a magnet for price.

00:05:12.358 --> 00:05:19.650
A sweep is a wick beyond the level with a fast return. A real breakout closes beyond it and holds on the retest.

00:05:19.650 --> 00:05:24.671
A sweep alone isn't enough. Wait for a change of character as confirmation.

00:05:24.671 --> 00:05:32.859
And don't put your stop where everyone else puts theirs. Risk only one to two percent of your account per trade.

